---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
import LearningDiagram from '@/components/modelling/LearningDiagram.astro';
import LearningIllustration from '@/components/modelling/LearningIllustration.astro';
import Callout from '@/components/Callout.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout
  title="Why shared meaning matters"
  description="Correctly formatted information can still answer the wrong question. Learn to read the subjects and connections that make an account understandable."
  proseClass="prose wide modelling-document learning-document"
>
  <section aria-labelledby="the-problem">
    <h2 id="the-problem"><span id="what" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="beyond-fields" aria-hidden="true"></span>A date is not yet an explanation</h2>
    <p>
      Imagine receiving “survey date: 20 August 2026” for Harbour Court. The date is
      readable and correctly formatted. Yet you cannot tell whether someone visited
      the dwelling that day, issued a report, corrected an earlier report or entered
      information into a system. The value may pass a format check while answering
      a different question from the one you intended to ask.
    </p>
    <p>
      In our fictional case, an inspection of Flat 1 occurred on 12 August. Report
      version 1 was issued on 14 August. Version 2, issued on 20 August, corrected the
      description of an access limitation; no second visit occurred. Calling all
      three dates “survey date” discards distinctions before information is exchanged.
      The problem is not the date format. It is the missing explanation of what each
      date describes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Semantic modelling</strong> is the work of making those meanings
      explicit: what we are describing, what we say about it, how it relates to other
      things and under which conditions a statement applies. An
      <a href="/glossary#ontology">ontology</a> formally expresses the selected
      distinctions. It does not replace professional knowledge; it gives that
      knowledge a form people can examine, challenge and use consistently.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="things">
    <h2 id="things"><span id="worked-example" aria-hidden="true"></span>The world, the agreement and a particular account</h2>
    <p>
      Keep three levels apart. There is a dwelling in the world. There is a model
      definition explaining what the model means by a dwelling. And there are
      particular records describing it. A definition can be clear while a record
      contains an error. A record can change while the dwelling remains the same.
      Neither a model nor a collection of records is the world itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Harbour Court is a building containing Flat 1 and Flat 2. An address description
      helps people locate or refer to a dwelling. A title record serves another
      purpose. A report describes an inspection and its findings. The appearance of
      “14 Orchard Road” in each description does not make these subjects
      interchangeable. Correcting an address's spelling does not create a dwelling;
      keeping its spelling does not prove that a later subdivision left the same
      dwelling in existence.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are questions about <a href="/glossary#resource">resources</a>—the
      identifiable things and concepts we describe—and their identity. A stable
      reference helps us talk about a subject repeatedly. It cannot decide, on its
      own, whether two descriptions concern the same subject.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="read-a-sentence">
    <h2 id="read-a-sentence"><span id="statement-caption" aria-hidden="true"></span>Read the connection as a sentence</h2>
    <p>
      Start without any diagram: <strong>Report version 2 describes the 12 August
      inspection.</strong> The first part tells us which subject we are talking about.
      “Describes” names the relationship. The last part tells us what that subject
      connects to. The diagram is another way of showing that sentence.
    </p>
    <LearningDiagram kind="sentence" caption="Start with the report, follow “describes”, and arrive at the inspection." />
    <p>
      Direction matters. Read from the arrow's starting point to its end. Reversing
      it would say that the inspection describes the report, which is not the
      claim intended here. The relationship name matters too: replacing “describes”
      with “is the same as” would make a different and unjustified claim.
      An unlabelled line leaves the reader to guess.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some sentences need more context than two names and a verb. “Alex is a buyer”
      leaves the arrangement unstated; “Alex participates as buyer in the proposed
      sale of Flat 1” identifies it. A relationship can also need a period, source
      or purpose. The first review task is to notice the missing meaning, not to
      choose how a technical specialist should encode it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="connected">
    <h2 id="connected"><span id="connected-caption" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="tree-graph" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="context-first" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="local-precision" aria-hidden="true"></span>One account, three useful representations</h2>
    <p>
      A form can be convenient for entering or reading a report. A nested description
      groups information beneath a chosen starting subject. A graph draws out
      connections across subjects. Each can express the same facts when its fields,
      references and relationships have clear meanings. Here is one deliberately
      complete account, shown three ways.
    </p>
    <LearningIllustration scene="technology" alt="Two differently arranged representations of a property situation, with the same relationship pieces kept between them." />
    <section class="learning-example document-flow" aria-labelledby="form-view">
      <h3 id="form-view">1. A readable form</h3>
      <dl>
        <dt>Report version</dt><dd>Version 2</dd>
        <dt>Issue date of this version</dt><dd>20 August 2026</dd>
        <dt>Earlier report revised</dt><dd>Version 1, issued 14 August 2026</dd>
        <dt>Inspection described by both versions</dt><dd>The inspection of Flat 1 on 12 August 2026</dd>
        <dt>Reason for the revision</dt><dd>Corrected description of an access limitation; no new visit.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>A form can preserve these connections. It needs sufficiently clear labels and references, not merely a field called “date”.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="learning-example document-flow" aria-labelledby="nested-view">
      <h3 id="nested-view">2. A nested description</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Report version 2
          <ul>
            <li>Issued: 20 August 2026.</li>
            <li>Revises: report version 1
              <ul><li>Issued: 14 August 2026.</li><li>Describes: the same 12 August inspection.</li></ul>
            </li>
            <li>Describes: the inspection
              <ul><li>Occurred: 12 August 2026.</li><li>Concerns: Flat 1.</li></ul>
            </li>
            <li>Correction: access-limitation wording; no new visit.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>The indentation chooses a reading order. The reference to the same inspection prevents the repeated description from implying a second event. A tree can contain references; nesting is not a prohibition on linking.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="learning-example document-flow" aria-labelledby="model-view">
      <h3 id="model-view">3. A connected account</h3>
      <LearningDiagram kind="representations" caption="The connected account keeps two report versions attached to one inspection of Flat 1." />
      <ol>
        <li>Report version 2 revises report version 1.</li>
        <li>Report version 2 describes the inspection that occurred on 12 August 2026.</li>
        <li>Report version 1 describes that same inspection.</li>
        <li>The inspection concerns Flat 1.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Version 1 was issued on 14 August and version 2 on 20 August. The correction concerns access-limitation wording, not a new visit. Those facts remain part of the account even when a small diagram concentrates on its connections.</p>
    </section>
    <p>
      The graph helps someone follow a subject across descriptions. It does not
      guarantee correct claims, automatically eliminate duplicates or prove that
      two similarly labelled nodes are identical. Equally, a familiar form is not
      inherently less meaningful. The test is what a recipient can understand after
      the information has moved between these representations.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="learning-example document-flow" aria-labelledby="try-it">
    <h2 id="try-it"><span id="ordinary-questions" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="participate" aria-hidden="true"></span>Practice · Follow three connections</h2>
    <p>
      Read “version 2 revises version 1”, “version 1 describes the inspection”, and
      “the inspection concerns Flat 1”. Which subjects do 20 August, 14 August and
      12 August describe? What must an exchange preserve so that its recipient does
      not infer a second visit?
    </p>
    <Callout title="Worked response" tone="method" heading="h3">
      <p>
        The first two dates are issue dates of different report versions. The third
        is the occurrence date of the inspection. Preserve the report-version
        distinction, the revision relationship, both links to the same inspection,
        the inspection's subject and the meanings of the dates. Preserve the source
        statement that the revision involved no new visit. A lone “latest date” value
        cannot carry that account.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <h3>Change the case</h3>
    <p>
      A fictional maintenance record was entered on 7 September and says: “Repair
      completed on 3 September. This is the first recording of that repair.”
      Someone proposes replacing the completion date with 7 September because it
      is later. What would you challenge?
    </p>
    <p>
      The repair completion and the recording are different subjects of time
      statements. Under the supplied evidence, the repair was completed on
      3 September and recorded on 7 September. Keep both meanings. If the later
      entry instead reported another repair, that event would need separate
      identification and evidence; lateness alone would not establish it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="benefits">
    <h2 id="benefits"><span id="why" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="less-reinterpretation" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="traceable-evidence" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="change" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="earn-benefits" aria-hidden="true"></span>The benefit comes from preserving a useful distinction</h2>
    <p>
      Fewer repeated explanations, traceable evidence and understandable corrections
      are worthwhile aims. They depend on maintained definitions and exchanges that
      preserve the agreement. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/understand/benefits-and-limits">benefits, limits and reasonable objections chapter</a>
      examines these conditions, costs and alternatives in full.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits"><span id="ai" aria-hidden="true"></span>Agreeing meaning is not centralising everybody's data</h2>
    <p>
      People can share definitions and identifiers without placing every property
      record in one database. A model does not prove that an inspection happened,
      grant access to a report or make an AI draft an approved standard.
      <a href="/semantic-modelling/understand/benefits-and-limits#accuracy-and-access">Accuracy, access and adoption need their own evidence</a>.
      Your useful contribution is a precise question about what the agreement says.
    </p>
    <p class="learning-note">
      If you make formal modelling decisions, the separate
      <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/classes-and-relationships">classes and relationships chapter</a>
      explains the technical representation. You do not need its syntax to review
      the sentences on this page.
    </p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
